{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EQWaMKKh2X1nz777Y6yAb2","type":"tool_result","content":"src/adapters/stacks/maven/java-imports-reader.ts:40:\treturn { sources: tree.files.map((f) => readDeclarations(f.path, f.text)), notes: tree.notes };\nsrc/adapters/stacks/maven/pitest-reader.ts:56:\t\t\ttext = await files.text(path);\nsrc/adapters/stacks/node/jest-reader.ts:132:\t\t\t(await run.reports(\"application/json\")).map((d) => d.text),\n}\n\n/**\n * The files of the workspace a reader may read, read by the runner: a path outside the copy is never\n * read, and a report past the read bound is returned unread with its size. A reader reads nothing on\n * its own, spawns nothing, writes nothing into the copy and reads no environment variable.\n */\nexport interface WorkspaceFiles {\n\t/** The report files the control declares; each one read is kept in the object store under `media_type`. */\n\treports(mediaType: string, reading?: ReportReading): Promise<ReportDocument[]>;\n\t/**\n\t * The text of the given files, keyed by path. A file absent or outside the copy has nothing to read;\n\t * one past the bound throws, an error rather than a silent gap.\n\t */\n\tsources(paths: readonly string[]): Promise<Map<string, string>>;\n\t/** The text of one file; it throws when the file is absent, unreadable or outside the copy. */\n\ttext(path: string): Promise<string>;\n\t/** The files whose name ends with `suffix` under the given workspace-relative roots, build output and caches aside. */\n\ttree(roots: readonly string[], suffix: string): Promise<SourceTree>;\n}\n\n/** What one run of a control hands the reader its definition names. */\nexport interface ReaderRun extends WorkspaceFiles {\n\tcontrol: ControlDefinition;\n\tobservation: ProcessObservation;\n\tstdout: string;\n\tstderr: string;\n\t/** What the subject introduced, line by line; null when nobody computed it. */\n\tintroduced_lines: IntroducedLines | null;\n\t/** The message without the workspace this run happened to use, so a finding pairs across the two trees. */\n\trelativize(message: string): string;\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-06T134711\n    date: \"2026-10-06\"\n    title: \"The review header invites `r` to reload on a newer candidate, and `r` does nothing in a real Pi\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: extension\n    what_happened: >\n      When a newer candidate exists than the one the review opened on, the header of the review surface reads\n      `newer candidate: cand_… (r)`. Pressing `r` reaches `handleKey`, which calls `actions.refresh()`, which calls\n      `onRefresh` — and `openReviewTui` in `src/extension/review-command.ts` never passes `onRefresh` to\n      `ReviewSurface`. The key is swallowed: the surface stays on the older candidate, with no message.\n    what_expected: >\n      Pressing `r` while the header announces a newer candidate reopens the review on that candidate, or the header\n      does not offer a key that does nothing.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Open `/495 review` on a change, let a newer candidate be frozen, and press `r` in the review: nothing changes.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      `SurfaceOptions.onRefresh` is optional (`src/presentation/tui/review-surface.ts`) and its only caller,\n      `openReviewTui`, omits it; no test of `test/v0-pure/review-surface.test.ts` presses `r`.\n    evidence: >\n      Read on 2026-10-06 at main 8bcf50d while checking the review surface against Pi's terminal UI guide: the only\n      references to `onRefresh` in `src/` are its declaration and its call in `review-surface.ts`.\n    risk_level: medium\n    follow_ups: >\n      Pass an `onRefresh` from `extension/` that ends the current interaction and opens the review on the newer\n      candidate, and hold it with a test that presses `r`.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-07T090000\n    date: \"2026-10-07\"\n    title: \"The status of a paused change reads like that of a change at work, and says the pause only in its Next line\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: presentation\n    what_happened: >\n      `/495 status` on a change paused during its specification shows the line `… Spécification · rédaction des\n      exigences`, the same line as a change running or ready in that step. The pause shows only as `Ensuite /495\n      resume` in the text, and as `status: paused` in the structured view.\n    what_expected: >\n      The verdict line of a paused change says that it is paused, and in which step, as the line of a change\n      waiting for a decision says that it waits.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Pause a change during its specification with `/495 pause`, then run `/495 status`: the verdict line reads `… Spécification · rédaction des exigences`.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      `verdictLine` in `src/presentation/structured/text.ts` has a branch for `decision_required`, for an accepted or\n      integrated outcome and for a stop (blocked, rejected, abandoned, cancelled); a `paused` status falls through to\n      the line of a change at work.\n    evidence: >\n      Read on 2026-10-07 in `verdictLine`, identical on main a7502e0; `test/v3-pi/resume.test.ts` holds the pause by\n      the view's `status` and the step by the text line. It predates the renaming of the phase identifiers.\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Give `verdictLine` a branch for `paused` that names the step, with a test on the text of a paused change.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-07T160000\n    date: \"2026-10-07\"\n    title: \"The PIT scope names an introduced path outside the copy as an unreadable source instead of a path that escapes the workspace\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: adapters\n    what_happened: >\n      `pitestScopeOf` (`src/adapters/stacks/maven/pitest-reader.ts`) reads each introduced Java source through\n      `WorkspaceFiles.text`, which throws `<path> escapes the workspace` for a path outside the copy. Th